From f0d1a4a473e79f83c71bddacb550ec14a3b86e13 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexander Penev <alexander.p.penev@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 11:33:36 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] compiler: emit the field in double, from the same codegen

CgCtx carries a ScalarKind and an fty(); the node bodies already went
through c.fc() and the type-following llvm_compat intrinsics, so they emit
f64 unchanged. emit_scene_sdf_f64 is the same walk as emit_scene_sdf with
one field different, which is the point - a second emitter could describe a
different solid, this cannot.

Measured against an analytic hollow cylinder at detector scale:

    worst |IR - analytic|    f32  6.73e-04 mm
                             f64  9.09e-13 mm

and against the interpreter, the f64 IR differs from evalw() on 1299 of
20000 points by at most 9.09e-13 mm. Not asserted bit-identical on purpose:
LLVM may contract a multiply and an add into one fma, which is a different
and better result than the interpreter's two roundings, and asserting
equality would be asserting that no optimiser ever improves the arithmetic.

The runtime parameter buffer stays float* - its ABI is shared with the
OpenCL and CUDA kernels - and an F64 emission widens what it loads. That
recovers nothing the buffer discarded, which is why the comment says so: a
runtime slot is f32-accurate by construction and an exact parameter must be
baked.

f64_blockers() names the nodes that cannot emit wide (MeshSdf's grid,
CustomExpr's own builder) before emission, using the same wide_eval() flag
eval/evalw use, rather than letting the IR verifier fail on a type mismatch.

AND A CORRECTION TO THE PREVIOUS COMMIT. It said scalar f32's slowness was
"structural rather than incidental". Compiled, f32 and f64 are EQUAL - 4.4
vs 4.5 ns, 0.97-0.99x over three runs - because the JIT folds parameters
into constants of the right type and the conversions the interpreter pays
are simply not there. The slowness is a property of the interpreter, not of
the type, and mixed_eval.hpp now says that.

That correction cost its own defect: the first version of this benchmark
converted points inside the timed loop, so every f32 call paid three
narrowings belonging to the harness and read as 1.29x SLOWER, which would
have confirmed the overstated claim. Each type now gets its points in its
own type. Fifth instance in this project of a benchmark measuring the
harness rather than the thing.

